Introduction to Time Complexity
Time complexity is a way to measure the efficiency of an algorithm. 
It tells us how the running time (or the number of steps executed) increases as the size of the input increases. 
By counting the number of basic operations (such as comparisons, arithmetic operations, or input/output actions), we can express the time complexity using notations like Big-O, which gives us a high-level understanding of the algorithm's performance under scaling.

Key Points:
Purpose: To determine how fast an algorithm runs relative to the input size.

Method: Count the number of basic operations (or steps) executed.

Representation: Usually expressed in Big-O notation (e.g., O(1), O(N), O(N²)), which provides an upper bound on the runtime.

Real-Life Examples
Sports Car vs. Auto-Rickshaw:
Imagine a race between a sports car and an auto-rickshaw.
The sports car wins because it has a superior speed and performance.
Similarly, in the world of algorithms, a faster (more efficient) algorithm will "win" by processing inputs more quickly than a slower one.

Google Search:
When you type a query into Google, the search engine processes billions of results and returns the answer in a fraction of a second. This speed is a direct result of highly optimized algorithms that can handle enormous amounts of data efficiently.
The Google returns results in about 0.25 seconds for around 6,18,00,00,000 results, emphasizing that even with huge data, efficiency is maintained through well-designed algorithms.
These examples illustrate that whether it's a race or a search query, the speed at which operations are carried out is crucial.
