Complexity Notations:
1)Big O Notation (O): (Worst Case Scenario)
Purpose: Describes the upper bound (worst-case scenario) of an algorithm’s running time.

How It Works: Constants and lower-order terms are ignored.

Examples:  

Algorithm 1: O(N)
Algorithm 2: O(1)
Algorithm 3: O(N²)

2)Big Theta Notation (Θ): (Average Case Scenario)
Purpose: Provides a tight bound, meaning it describes the exact asymptotic behavior (both upper and lower bounds) of an algorithm’s running time.

Usage: When an algorithm’s performance can be bounded both above and below by the same function.

3)Big Omega Notation (Ω): (Best Case Scenario)
Purpose: Describes the lower bound (best-case scenario) of an algorithm’s running time.

Usage: It guarantees that the algorithm will take at least a certain amount of time.

Fundamental Rules in Complexity Analysis:
 i ) __ Neglect Constants __:  When analyzing time complexity, constant factors (like the “+2” in 3N + 2) do not affect the growth rate.
 ii) __ Ignore Lower Order Terms __: For large input sizes, the highest order term dominates. For instance, in Algorithm 3, the term 3N² grows much faster than 5N or 2.

Summary:
Algorithm 1 (3N + 2):  
Linear growth; worst-case running time is O(N).

Algorithm 2 (2):  
Constant time; worst-case running time is O(1).

Algorithm 3 (3N² + 5N + 2):  
Quadratic growth; worst-case running time is O(N²).

